Marfan's syndrome (Acute Cardiovascular Complication Risk Assessment)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Disproportionate tall stature/Arachnodactyly, Ectopia lentis, Aortic root dilatation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-029
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, tearing mid-back pain in a young adult with recognized Marfanoid habitus, raising immediate clinical suspicion for an acute aortic event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and flatmate
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a tall, thin young adult experiencing a sudden onset of severe, unremitting, "tearing" pain between the shoulder blades, completely unresponsive to position changes or analgesia. Urgent, on-site clinical assessment was strictly mandated to rapidly evaluate for acute aortic dissection—a known, life-threatening complication of Marfan syndrome—and to facilitate immediate, stabilized emergency transport if clinical suspicion remained high.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My back is ripping apart; it started suddenly an hour ago right between my shoulder blades, and it feels like a hot knife tearing downwards."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an explosive onset of severe, unrelenting mid-thoracic back pain. Timeline: Patient was watching television 60 minutes ago when struck by a sudden, severe, tearing sensation in the mid-back that radiated inferiorly. The pain reached maximal intensity instantly. Pain is 10/10, constant, and totally unresponsive to positional changes. The patient reports a known history of generalized joint hypermobility, severe progressive scoliosis requiring previous bracing, and acknowledges having an arm span wider than their height. Denies any recent trauma, heavy lifting, or fever. Reports mild nausea and profound diaphoresis.
Functional Status: Acutely distressed, writhing on the sofa, unable to find a comfortable position.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening medical emergency.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 168/95 (Right Arm) | 142/80 (Left Arm) | HR: 110 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 24 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, pale, profoundly diaphoretic, in extreme visible agony.
Physical Exam: Marfanoid habitus is clearly evident: tall, disproportionate stature with long, slender extremities. Arachnodactyly noted with a visually positive Steinberg (thumb) sign. Anterior chest wall reveals a mild pectus excavatum. Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular tachycardia; a faint diastolic murmur is audible at the right sternal border (suggestive of acute aortic regurgitation). Crucial finding: Significant blood pressure differential (>20 mmHg systolic) noted between the right and left arms. Radial pulses are bounding on the right, but distinctly diminished and delayed on the left. Neurological exam of the lower extremities shows intact motor and sensory function currently. Respiratory exam is clear bilaterally.
Environmental Observations: N/A - Primary focus is on immediate medical stabilization.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications. Took Paracetamol 30 mins ago with zero effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Acute Thoracic Aortic Dissection secondary to Marfan Syndrome phenotype - Status: CRITICAL EMERGENCY
Differentials:

Acute Myocardial Infarction - Unlikely given the specific "tearing" quality of the pain radiating to the back and the profound pulse deficits, but remains a critical differential requiring ECG confirmation.

Musculoskeletal Back Spasm - Definitively ruled out by the explosive onset, "tearing" quality, profound autonomic distress (diaphoresis), and the critical presence of unequal blood pressures and pulse deficits.

Pulmonary Embolism - Less likely given the primary focus of pain in the mid-back rather than pleuritic chest pain, and the presence of upper extremity pulse deficits, though hypoxia can occur.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme/Life-Threatening

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a textbook clinical picture of an acute aortic dissection: explosive, tearing back pain, a significant blood pressure differential between arms, and a new diastolic murmur in a patient with a clear Marfanoid habitus (arachnodactyly, pectus excavatum). The underlying connective tissue fragility inherently predisposes this patient to aortic root dilatation and catastrophic rupture. Any delay in diagnosis or attempt at conservative management would be universally fatal. The urgent home visit rapidly recognized the critical vascular nature of the pain, bypassing any musculoskeletal misdiagnosis, and allowed for the immediate activation of high-priority emergency medical services for rapid transport to a cardiothoracic surgical center, alongside immediate on-site stabilization.

